Generating meshes using gmsh
----------------------------

Some examples use meshes generated by gmsh.

Gmsh uses geometry files with the extension .geo. The mesh (in the mesh.msh
file) can be changed by changing the mesh.geo file and running
   
    gmsh -1 mesh.geo  

for line geometries (including 1D meshes) or

    gmsh -2 mesh.geo  

for surface geometries (including 2D meshes) or

    gmsh -3 mesh.geo 

for volume geometries (3D), where it is assumed that all other non-default
mesh generation settings (such as the required order of the elements and the
meshing algorithm) have been set in the file mesh.geo. Omitting the -1, -2 or
-3 option, the geometry and mesh can be changed interactively.

To promote modularity and reuse of code, we usually split
the geometry file into a small main geometry file (with the extension .geo)
and a rather general geometry file (having the extension .igo). The mesh.geo
file contains mainly parameters that can be changed and then "Includes" the
mesh.igo after the setting of the parameters. The mesh.igo file depends
on the parameters and therefore is also incomplete (.igo means either
"incomplete geo" or "include geo"). In order for the mesh.igo file being
recognized as a .geo file, it should preferably have the following last line:

// vim: set filetype=gmsh :  //

In summary:

mesh.geo: File containing parameters that can be changed.
mesh.igo: The general geometry file that is more or less fixed for a problem.
          Included by mesh.geo after the parameters have been set.
mesh.msh: the generated mesh.


Adaptive meshing using gmsh
---------------------------

It is possible to use gmsh for adaptive meshing. For this, a "background mesh"
with a scalar field representing the local element size is needed. A parsed
format file (.pos) is needed to specify the scalar field. The "scalar.pos" file
can be generated form a tfem program by writing a sysvector or vector to
the file using the routine write_scalar_gmsh_parsed. The new mesh can be
generated from the command line, for example:

    gmsh -2 mesh.geo -bgm scalar.pos


Postprocessing using gmsh
-------------------------

It is possible to use gmsh for postprocessing. The datafiles are in the MSH
fileformat. The files define the data fields in addition to the mesh. We
recommend using the .mso file extension to reserve .msh for a mesh only (.mso
stands for msh output).


Gmsh4 new file version not yet supported
----------------------------------------

Gmsh4 has introduced a new msh file format (version 4) and also made
that the default one. This new format is not yet supported by tfem.
In order to use gmsh4 do one of the following
  - Run gmsh with the command
       gmsh -format msh2 ...
  - or put a line 
      Mesh.MshFileVersion = 2.2;
    in the geo file.
  - or edit or create the file $HOME/.gmsh-options and put a line
      Mesh.MshFileVersion = 2.2;
    in the file. Note, that this option is "semi-permanent" in the sense that
    any gmsh session outputs a version 2.2 file until you remove the line.

Gmsh4 Periodic meshes
---------------------

For higher-order (>=2) meshes Gmsh4 introduced an optimization step for
positioning the nodes for periodic meshes. At first, this introduced an
incompatibility between gmsh3 and gmsh4 for our periodic mesh geo files, see:
  http://onelab.info/pipermail/gmsh/2018/012587.html
and
  http://onelab.info/pipermail/gmsh/2018/012672.html
Starting from gmsh4.0.6 the optimization step has been made optional for now.
The optimization step can be invoked by setting
  Mesh.HighOrderPeriodic = 1;
or turned off by
  Mesh.HighOrderPeriodic = 0;
The default value is 0, for now. 
Periodic geo files can be made compatible with the high-order optimization step
by explicitly specifying the transformation between periodic curves and
surfaces, for example a Translate. All geo files in tfem have been modified
to be compatible with the optimizer.
